        "original_id": "91c1ac3a-5dff-5794-a3e7-e2351e603498",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Claude Simon, Paul Auster, Alain Robbe-Grillet, Nathalie Sarraute, Michel Butor\n- Topics: Nobel Prize in Literature, Nouveau Roman, absurdism, existentialism, crime fiction, identity, personal meaning\n\nFacts:\n- Claude Simon was born on 10 October 1913 and died on 6 July 2005.\n- Paul Auster was born on February 3, 1947.\n- Alain Robbe-Grillet was one of the figures most associated with the Nouveau Roman trend of the 1960s, along with Nathalie Sarraute, Michel Butor, and Claude Simon.\n- Claude Simon was a French novelist and critic.\n- Paul Auster is an American writer and director whose writing blends absurdism, existentialism, crime fiction, and the search for identity and personal meaning.\n- Claude Simon received the 1985 Nobel Prize in Literature.\n- Paul Auster's books have been translated into more than forty languages.",
